h higher engine capacity and novel propulsion systems.\n\ndf['Engine_Propulsion_Interaction'] = df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'] * df['Propulsion_Code']\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3: Rolling Mean of Casualties per Police Force (Temporal Aggregation)  \npython\n# Feature name: Casualties_Per_PoliceForce_RollingMean\n# Feature description: Compute the rolling mean of the number of casualties grouped by Police_Force. This feature aggregates past casualty data for each reporting police force over time, smoothing volatility and aiding spatial analysis.\n# Usefulness: Some police forces may operate in areas that consistently experience higher casualty counts (more urban activity or population density) and might be related to specific driver sex demographics for such regions.\n\ndf['Casualties_Per_PoliceForce_RollingMean'] = df.groupby('Police_Force')['Number_of_Casualties'].transform(lambda x: x.rolling(window=3, min_periods=1).mean())\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropped Columns-Based Reasoning\n\n#### Drop Highly Skewed Columns with Minimal Variation\npython\n# Explanation why the column Vehicle_Location-Restricted_Lane is dropped\n# The Vehicle_Location-Restricted_Lane column has a highly repetitive value (Median = Q3 = Q1 = 0), indicating minimal variation in the data. This column may not provide useful features for predicting driver's sex.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['Vehicle_Location-Restricted_Lane'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Drop Irrelevant Columns Featuring Dictionary-Like Attributes\npython\n# Explanation why the column Local_Authority_(District) is dropped\n# The Local_Authority_(District) is likely a categorical geographic identifier, but it has an extremely large range (1-940) that lacks significant relationship to sex. Including it might cause noise or overfitting without meaningful representation.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['Local_Authority_(District)'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\nThe added features Age_Driver_Vehicle_Interaction, Engine_Propulsion_Interaction, and Casual